
==============================================================================
1. A constant rate from age 0: the accumulator and by_clock are identical
==============================================================================
   rate 1 / 3: identical over 20000 self-creations: True; the accumulator is (age + 1) n mod d, checked: True
   rate 32 / 55: identical over 20000 self-creations: True; the accumulator is (age + 1) n mod d, checked: True
   rate 4198400 / 4198400: identical over 20000 self-creations: True; the accumulator is (age + 1) n mod d, checked: True
   rate 1836 / 1048576: identical over 20000 self-creations: True; the accumulator is (age + 1) n mod d, checked: True
   rate 7 / 1: identical over 20000 self-creations: True; the accumulator is (age + 1) n mod d, checked: True
   rate 1023 / 1024: identical over 20000 self-creations: True; the accumulator is (age + 1) n mod d, checked: True
   rate 9715512228193 / 291465366845793: identical over 20000 self-creations: True; the accumulator is (age + 1) n mod d, checked: True
   proof: after k self-creations acc = k n - count x d with 0 <= acc < d, so count = floor(k n / d): the same whole part, the remainder kept instead of recomputed.

==============================================================================
2. A start at a nonzero age, or a changed rate: where they differ
==============================================================================
   start age  1, acc 0: per-step differences in [-1, 0, 1], the sum 0; with acc initialised to a0 n mod d = 32: identical True
   start age  7, acc 0: per-step differences in [-1, 0, 1], the sum 0; with acc initialised to a0 n mod d = 4: identical True
   start age 30, acc 0: per-step differences in [-1, 0, 1], the sum 0; with acc initialised to a0 n mod d = 25: identical True
   a changed rate: by_clock at the CURRENT rate reads floor(age n' / d) - floor((age - 1) n' / d), a count that forgets the old rate's whole part
   rate 32/55 for 60 steps then 8/55: by_clock's Links 43 (the first 60: 34, the next 60: 9, longest burst of consecutive 1s after the change 1),
   the accumulator's 43 = floor((60 x 32 + 60 x 8) / 55) = 43: the distance the rate drove, exactly, no burst.

==============================================================================
3. A varying numerator (the push): per interval within 1 of each other, the sums
==============================================================================
   5000 intervals, X_t uniform in [0, 5 D], D = 1000: the largest per-interval difference 1 (each is floor(X_t / D) or one more);
   the accumulator's sum 12239 = floor(sum X_t / D) = 12239 exactly, the remainder 295 in the accumulator;
   by_clock's sum 12278: off the exact 12239.3 by +38.7 (a drift with no owner; over T intervals it can reach T in the worst case).
   an adversarial X_t below D chosen to round up at every age (2000 intervals): by_clock counts 1998, the exact sum X_t / D = 1998.0, the accumulator 1998.

==============================================================================
4. Additivity: one accumulator takes the directions in any order, exactly
==============================================================================
   trial 0: 290 directions summed one by one 454 (acc 1985), shuffled 454 (acc 1985), all at once 454 (acc 1985): equal True; the per-direction floors sum to 318, short by 136 units (at most 289)
   trial 1: 290 directions summed one by one 447 (acc 2947), shuffled 447 (acc 2947), all at once 447 (acc 2947): equal True; the per-direction floors sum to 299, short by 148 units (at most 289)
   trial 2: 290 directions summed one by one 399 (acc 57), shuffled 399 (acc 57), all at once 399 (acc 57): equal True; the per-direction floors sum to 259, short by 140 units (at most 289)
